{"name":"Britches","hex":"#a09073","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"britches","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of breeches (pants, trousers).","The roe of cod."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/britches"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breeches","blocks":[{"text":"Breeches or britches are an article of clothing covering the body from the waist down, with separate coverings for each leg, usually stopping just below the knee, though in some cases reaching to the ankles. Formerly a standard item of Western men's clothing, they had fallen out of use by the mid-19th century in favour of trousers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Elijah_Boardman_MET_DT60.jpg/330px-Elijah_Boardman_MET_DT60.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breeches"},{"title":"University of California, Riverside 1985 laboratory raid","blocks":[{"text":"In 1985, a raid took place at a laboratory belonging to the University of California, Riverside (UCR) that resulted in the removal of a monkey by the Animal Liberation Front (ALF). This monkey, called Britches, was a stump-tailed macaque who was born into a breeding colony at UCR. He was removed from his mother at birth, had his eyelids sewn shut, and had an electronic sonar device attached to his head—a Trisensor Aid, an experimental version of a blind travel aid, the Sonicguide—as part of a three-year sensory-deprivation study involving 24 infant monkeys. The experiments were designed to study the behavioral and neural development of monkeys reared with a sensory substitution device."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/BritchesALF-cropped.jpg/330px-BritchesALF-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California%2C_Riverside_1985_laboratory_raid"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Koninklijk_Koetsier_2013_74.JPG/500px-Koninklijk_Koetsier_2013_74.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Koninklijk_Koetsier_2013_74.JPG/500px-Koninklijk_Koetsier_2013_74.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Breeches are still worn as livery for special occasions in several European courts. Here, a coachman in the Netherlands wears them during Prinsjesdag, 2013.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoninklijk_Koetsier_2013_74.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b7/Knee_Breches_buckles.jpg/500px-Knee_Breches_buckles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b7/Knee_Breches_buckles.jpg/500px-Knee_Breches_buckles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pair of buckles for dress breeches. The T-hook of the buckle is inserted into a buttonhole on the strap at the bottoms of the leg of the breeches. The end of the strap is slipped through, the prongs lowered and then the end slipped through the other side of the buckle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKnee_Breches_buckles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg/500px-Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg/1280px-Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg/500px-Henry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Equestrian portrait of Prince Henry, Duke of Cumberland and Strathearn by David Morier around 1765.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenry_Duke_of_Cumberland_by_David_Morier_1765.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg/500px-Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg/1280px-Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg/500px-Horse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"2005 Melbourne show competitor in jodhpurs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHorse_riding_in_coca_cola_arena_-_melbourne_show_2005.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Wistar_rat.jpg/500px-Wistar_rat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Wistar_rat.jpg/1280px-Wistar_rat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Wistar_rat.jpg/500px-Wistar_rat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWistar_rat.jpg"}],"sounds":{"britches":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/En-us-britches.ogg?utm_source=en.wikt